{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WmsQCF8LJwe3uL54Dpspz2","type":"tool_result","content":"307\t- `E.ui.box/bar/hearts` draw classic windows and meters inside `r.overlay`.\n308\t- `Dialog` does typewriter text with blips, pages, speaker names and choices; `update` returns true while it is open, so the world waits. Its markup gives a word its color (`{c=#ffd36a}gold{/c}`), a beat before the next line (`{w=.5}`), a slow dread (`{s=12}...{/s}`), a scream that shakes (`{shake}`) or a song that bobs (`{wave}`), and the hero's name (`{$name}` from `vars`).\n309\t- `Menu` handles up and down with key repeat, confirm, cancel and disabled items.\n310\t\n311\t### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\n312\tA modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild) wants readable HTML screens over the pixel world, not pixel windows. `E.Shell` builds them from data, so you write none of the HTML, CSS or focus handling. It shows the game's state and holds none of it: you pass values in each step and callbacks for the buttons. The STEALTH slice (`src/starter/70-stealth.js`: the objective, health, status, the takedown prompt, toasts, the results) and the template game use it; copy from them.\n313\t- `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: { ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont }, settings: { items: [{ key: 'cones', label: 'Sight cones', value: true }], onChange(s, key) {} }, screens: { pause: {...}, help: {...} } })`. It takes over from the engine's own and keeps the standard menu (see Standard features). Your items join `game.settings` (`ui.settings` is the same object), saved and checked with the rest; `onChange` hears every change.\n314\t- Screens: `ui.show(name, spec)`, `ui.back()`, `ui.hide()`, `ui.screen` (the open one's name, or null). Every spec takes `kicker`, `title`, `text` (strings or functions), `actions: [[label, fn], { label, act, primary, disabled }]` (falsy entries are skipped, so `cond && [...]` works; `act` taps an input action) and `back`. Then: `'title'` (`name`, `subtitle`, `meta`, `footer`), `'pause'` (Resume first, then your actions, then Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help and Quit to title; it opens while `game.paused` and Resume unpauses; `screens: { pause: false }` leaves it out), `'settings'`, `'controls'`, `'help'` (`keys: [[key, what]]`, `sections: [[heading, text]]`), `'results'` (`emblem`, `stats: [[value, label]]`, `medals: [[label, earned]]`) and `'map'` (`map`, `at`, `markers`, `labels`, `onPick(x, y)` for a waypoint). A screen opened from another stacks; cancel goes back.\n315\t- The HUD, each step: `ui.hud({ objective: { step, title, text, at, from }, status, bars: [{ label, value, max, kind: 'pips' | 'bar', color }], counts: [[label, value]], prompt: { key, label, hint, progress }, tip, place, keys, buttons: [[label, fn]], minimap: { map, at, markers, onClick } })`. A piece left out is hidden, only what changed is redrawn, and the HUD hides while a screen is open. `objective` shows the distance in meters (`meters: .125` a world unit) and whether the target is above or below. `ui.toast(title, text, seconds)` and `ui.tutorial(title, text, seconds)` come and go on their own.\n316\t- Input: the game's own input moves the focus (keys, d-pad, stick: up / down / left / right, a slider or a choice turns left and right), confirm presses, cancel goes back, pause closes the menu; touch taps. While a screen is open the game's actions are held back, so a menu press doesn't also attack. Touch players reach the menu with the touch START or the corner's button; `buttons: [['Map', openMap]]` adds your own.\n317\t- It honors reduced motion and the safe-area insets, is headless-safe (it keeps its screens' names and draws nothing), and `game.dispose()` takes it away.\n318\t\n319\t### Lighting\n320\t- Light every scene (the Look target's step 9): a dark game with no lights reads flat, and a judge says so. Canvas lighting leads because it draws the same in every browser and every headless check: set `lights.enabled` and `ambient` (.4-.6 for dusk and interiors) in the scene's `enter`, then call `lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color })` every frame where light would come from (lamps, torches, windows, the objective's glow). Light is measured on the ground in ground views, and on screen in side views and for PlatformMaps. The template game and the STEALTH slice show it. Its pass costs a few milliseconds a frame at 320x240 (more on a phone): a heavy scene can keep it for a computer, `matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches`, as the STEALTH slice does.\n321\t- GPU lighting (`game.enableGPU({ map })`) needs WebGPU and a TileMap. It adds:\n322\t  - colored lights, and soft shadows cast by walls and `lights.caster(...)`;\n323\t  - light that wraps around characters;\n324\t  - glow from `px.glow`, emissive decals and actors, and `[r, g, b, glow]` floor texels;\n325\t  - bloom and heat shimmer (`lights.heat`).\n326\t- Where GPU lighting can't draw (no WebGPU, still loading, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with `lights.ambient` and the same lights: set both ambients (`gpu.ambient = [r, g, b]`, `lights.ambient`) so the two look alike (the ADVENTURE slice does); `gpu.fallback = false` leaves such frames unlit. Tune the settings, not the shaders.\n327\t\n328\t### Errors and warnings\n329\t- Runtime errors appear in a red box (click to hide) and in the console, labeled with `update` or `draw` and the scene.\n330\t- Setup errors (before `game.start`) are shown too.\n331\t- One-time `my-3D2dge:` warnings flag:\n332\t  - unknown views, actions, sounds, songs, resolutions and input presets;\n333\t  - legend characters, wall or tile types missing from `types`;\n334\t  - uneven level rows and non-hex colors;\n335\t  - lights with bad numbers, and PlatformMaps drawn in the wrong view.\n336\t- The checker prints them all.\n337\t\n338\t### Testing without a browser\n339\tA game runs in Node with no screen, so its rules can be tested in a second, exactly:\n340\t- `new E.Game()` is headless where there is no DOM (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, renderer, sound or DOM input; scenes update and `draw` is skipped. Sprites made while the game loads get a stand-in canvas.\n341\t- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates. In a page, `new E.Game({ loop: false })` waits for `advance` too, and `advance(s, { draw: true })` draws a frame after.\n342\t- Input: `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step (`pressed` and `down` in the next step, `released` in the one after); `hold('right')` holds it until `hold('right', false)`; `press(code)` and `release(code)` press keys.\n343\t- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return { scene, hero, score } }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }`: your game's handle. Tests read it; `tools/check.mjs` prints it after play; `tools/replay.mjs` logs it at the end of a replay.\n344\t- `game.stop()` stops the loop, `game.start()` resumes it, `game.dispose()` ends the game and removes every listener the engine added to the page.\n345\t\n346\t```js\n347\t// game/test.mjs in the handoff package: load the engine and the game into one context, then drive it\n348\tconst ctx = { console }; vm.createContext(ctx);\n349\tfor (const f of ['../my-3d2dge.js', './game.js']) vm.runInContext(readFileSync(new URL(f, import.meta.url), 'utf8'), ctx);\n350\tconst game = ctx.My3D2dge.current;\n351\tconst I = game.input; I.tap('start'); game.advance(.5);\n352\tgame.debug.teleport(100, 88, 0, 0); I.tap('jump'); game.advance(.1);   // facing the roof's wall: jump takes hold of it\n353\tI.hold('up'); game.advance(.6); I.hold('up', false); game.advance(1);   // up the screen is up the wall: it pulls up at the top\n354\tif (game.debug.snapshot().hero[2] !== 32) throw new Error('the hero should have climbed onto the roof');\n355\t```\n356\t\n357\t### The handoff package\n358\t`dist/handoff/` (on the site: `/handoff/`; `node tools/handoff.mjs --zip <file>` zips it for a chat) holds what a coding agent needs to build on the full engine without the repository: both editions, each module on its own (`modules/`), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, this guide, the tools that check, film and replay a game, the asset card with its tools and the store, a `package.json` pinning Playwright, `game/` (a template game with a headless test) and `kits/` (each 2D genre kit's game, readable, with the page that plays it and a headless test). Its `README.md` says where to start; `tools/build.mjs` writes it from `src/handoff/`. It leaves out the 3D engine and its kits (on the site and in the repository).\n359\t\n360\t### Modules (general systems in files of their own)\n361\tA module (`engine/modules/<name>.js`) holds one general system that several genres share, outside the core, so that only the games that use it carry it. It loads after the engine, in a `<script>` of its own, and attaches its names to `My3D2dge`; its header is its manual (what it holds, its API, a complete example, its rules), and the index has a section for it. Its manual's third line says whether it also loads after the agent edition, and which modules it needs first.\n362\t- **In a single file:** the reference editions (`dist/my-3d2dge.html`, the compact one) carry every module; a kit carries the modules its slice names in its header (`@modules grid gen`). To use one a kit hasn't got, paste the module's file in a `<script>` after the engine's (if the console then names a section the kit's engine left out, start from the reference edition).\n363\t- **With files:** `dist/my-3d2dge.js` (`my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package) is the core and every module in one file; the handoff package also has each module on its own (`modules/<name>.js`), to load after the agent edition (the twelve that can).\n364\t- **The modules:** `grid` (square and hex grids: A* paths, move ranges with climb and drop past allies, lines, symmetric fields of view, `E.Fog`, flood fills and groups, tactics' shapes of effect, facing and threat maps, `E.GridMover` for Pac-Man's and Sokoban's moves, `E.Turns` by speed, charge time, phase or ATB), `gen` (floors from a seed: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes, Poisson scatter, as ASCII rows a `TileMap` reads, with `E.gen.mark` for spawns), `rooms` (a world of rooms on a grid of screens: doors at open edges, a slide or a fade between them, the explored map, gates and saves: `E.World`), `arcade` (the coin-op's frame: a score that floats up where it was won, extra lives at thresholds, two players taking turns, credits and a continue, high-score tables with initials on a letter wheel, stage cards, the attract mode), `mode7` (Super Mario Kart's and F-Zero's view: a camera over a flat floor drawn a row at a time, the sky turning with it, billboards scaled by distance, `E.Mode7`) and `road` (OutRun's road: segments with curves, hills and tunnels, scenery and cars scaled by distance, `E.Road`) and `drive` (cars with grip, drift and the ground's tag, races with laps, checkpoints, drivers and items: `E.Car`, `E.Race`), `story` (dialogue as data: lines, choices with conditions, flags, quests, events, emote balloons: `E.Story`), `rpg` (items, bags, members with levels and gear, status, a party, shops, damage, encounters, windows: `E.RPG`), `physics` (2D rigid bodies: circles, boxes, polygons, chains; flippers, bumpers, kickers: `E.Physics`), `terrain` (destructible ground in the side plane: carve, walk, ray: `E.Terrain`) and `songs` (the songbook: `E.Songbook`): these twelve load after the agent edition too (a race's `E.Path` is the full engine's). Four load after the full engine only: `fight` (one-on-one fighting: `E.Fighter`, `E.Match`, `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`, training), `click` (point-and-click: `E.Click`, `E.Walkbox`), `rhythm` (`E.Rhythm`: charts, judgments, holds, calls) and `strategy` (`E.Strategy`, `E.Clock`, `E.needs`; it needs `grid`).\n365\t- **Writing one:** `engine/modules/README.md` (the header's form, the rules, its budget of 1,500 tokens; `tools/modules-test.mjs` proves the path with a fixture). `docs/KITS.md` says which systems become modules, and what every kit has.\n366\t\n367\t### Kits and the diet (which file for which game)\n368\tA genre kit (`dist/kits/my-3d2dge-<genre>.html`) is the engine, the API card and one slice, sized for a chat model's context: a 2D kit is at most 200,000 tokens, six of them up to about 241,000 (`doctrine-check` counts them). The handoff package's `kits/` holds each 2D kit's game readable, on the full engine. Its engine is on the **kit diet**: the core less the sections its game never calls (the tile map in the platformer's kit, the third-person kit and GPU lighting in most), as the kit's header lists. Calling a left-out name warns once in the console, naming its section (a constructor throws). So:\n369\t- **A game in the kit's genre:** start from the kit; anything its slice uses is there.\n370\t- **A game of several genres** (a platformer with a top-down overworld, an RPG with a shoot-'em-up stage), or one that needs a section a kit left out: start from `dist/my-3d2dge.html` (every slice and section, readable) or `dist/my-3d2dge-compact.html` (the same, minified), or `dist/my-3d2dge.js` with your own page.\n371\t- **The presentation sections** (Penner's easings beyond the core's, sprite frames and turns, tweens and scripts, paths, transitions, weather, input sequences, the camera's window and framing) are each a part of the diet: a kit carries the ones its slice uses, and a kit whose slice uses none carries about 1,600 tokens of them in its engine (the screen filter, a standard setting, and the small ways in) and 1,000 in its card.\n372\t- **Its proof:** each slice has `run(n)` (`__game.scenes.<id>.run(600)`): n fixed steps of scripted play from a seed, returning a hash that is the same every time; `node kits/test.mjs` in the handoff package runs each kit's twice, headless (the repository's `tools/kits-test.mjs`, in a browser). A game copied from a slice can keep one: the shell's `proof()` is how.\n373\t\n374\t### Recorded sessions (a bug from a phone, replayed exactly)\n375\t- `E.session.record(true)` turns recording on for every later page load (`?record=1` does it for one load). It starts before the game's code and keeps everything a game's next frame depends on:\n376\t  - a seed for `Math.random`;\n377\t  - each display frame's time (`performance.now` and `Date.now` read it during that frame);\n378\t  - every input and the frame it arrived before: keys, pointer and touch, wheel, clicks and form edits, focus, visibility, resize, full screen;\n379\t  - gamepad readings, the media queries the game asked, the safe-area insets, the screen, and the save (`localStorage`) at the start;\n380\t  - a checksum every 60 frames.\n381\t- `E.session.share()` saves the session: the share sheet on a phone, a download elsewhere. In Emberdeep it is Settings → Tools → Record a session; on any recorded page the standard pause menu has Save session (a phone has no console). `node tools/replay.mjs <file> --pacing` reads the device's refreshes from it without replaying: the refresh, the ones missed second by second, how unevenly (a report of choppy play).\n382\t- `node tools/replay.mjs session.json` replays it in headless Chromium at the recorded screen size, frame by frame, and reports the first checksum that differs. Useful options:\n383\t  - `--to 1800` stops at a frame; `--shots 600,1200` saves screenshots;\n384\t  - `--log \"My3D2dge.current.sceneName\"` prints any state at the end (a game's `game.debug.snapshot()` prints by itself);\n385\t  - `--page` replays on another page. Fix the bug, rebuild, and replay the same session to see it gone.\n386\t- `game.stateHash = () => [hero.x, hero.y, hero.hp, foes.length]` adds a game's own state to the checksum, so a replay stops at the first frame where that state differs.\n387\t- Why it works: the clock turns the same frame times into the same fixed steps, so the same frame times, inputs and seed give the same game.\n388\t- Any browser: while recording and replaying, `Math.sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `atan`, `atan2`, `asin`, `acos`, `exp`, `log`, `log2`, `log10`, `pow`, `hypot` and `cbrt` are the engine's portable versions (`E.session.math`).\n389\t  - Browsers may round these differently in the last bit: an iPhone's Safari and Chromium use different libraries.\n390\t  - The portable versions are built only from `+ - * /` and `sqrt`, which are exact everywhere.\n391\t  - So a session recorded in one browser replays in another bit for bit.\n392\t  - `sin`, `cos`, `atan`, `atan2`, `exp` and `log` are fdlibm's algorithms, the same bits as Chromium's own.\n393\t  - The others are within a few units in the last place.\n394\t  - `node tools/cross-engine-math.cjs` checks this in Safari's own engine. It runs the same code in V8 and in JavaScriptCore, through Bun (`BUN=/path/to/bun`).\n395\t  - Native math gives different bits in the two engines, and so does a Humanoid animated with it.\n396\t  - The portable math, and the same Humanoid with it, give the same bits in both.\n397\t- Limits:\n398\t  - the GPU lighting is off while recording, because it starts up on real time;\n399\t  - a session is one page load;\n400\t  - replay on the build it was recorded on: its version and build are in the file.\n401\t\n402\t## Genre recipes\n403\tEach recipe names the kit to start from (`kits/<id>.js` in the handoff package). The 3D ones build on the 3D engine, whose kits are on the site and in the repository, not in the handoff package.\n404\t\n405\t**Side-scroller (Mario, Mega Man, Castlevania).**\n406\t```js\n407\tconst level = new E.PlatformMap({ rows: LEVEL, legend: { '#': 1, '=': 2, '?': 3, '@': 'hero', 'g': 'walker' },\n408\t  types: { 1: { style: 'ground', side: '#8a5a32' }, 2: { kind: 'oneway' }, 3: { style: 'bonus', side: '#e8a830' } } });\n409\tconst s = level.find('hero'), hero = new E.Platformer({ x: s.x, z: s.z }), rig = new E.Humanoid({ weapon: 'gun', hat: 'cap' });\n410\t// scene: { view: 'side', views: ['side', 'brawler'], input: 'PLATFORMER', res: 'snes', pausable: true, ... }\n411\t// update: hero.update(dt, level, game.input, lifts); if (hero.jumped) game.audio.sfx('jump');\n412\t//         if (hero.bumped) { ...change level.set(cx, cz, id)... }\n413\t//         rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ point: game.input.down('attack') })); game.focus(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 24);\n414\t// draw:   r.sky([...]); level.draw(r); r.actor(hero.x, 0, hero.z, (g, ox, oy) => rig.draw(g, ox, oy, r.view));\n415\t```\n416\t- Enemies are boxes moved by `level.move(e, dt)` with your own gravity: turn at `hitWall`, and at ledges using `groundBelow`.\n417\t- Stomp when `hero.vz < 0` and the hero's feet are above the enemy's middle.\n418\t- Pits: `hero.z < -40`. Spikes: `level.touching(hero, 'hazard')`.\n419\t- Further moves, on request: `new E.Platformer({ crouch: true, ledge: true, wallCling: true, momentum: true })`; tiles of `kind: 'water' | 'rope' | 'bars'`, and grip on any tile type: `friction` (ice: .15), `carry` (a conveyor's speed), `bounce` (a spring), `crumble` with `respawn` (call `level.update(dt)` each step); `hero.swingFrom(x, z)` hangs it from a grapple point. Draw `level.drawWater(r)` after the actors; `rigState()` poses every move (hang, climb, crawl, swim, swing).\n420\t\n421\t**Metroidvania (Metroid, Super Metroid, Symphony of the Night).** Start from the METROIDVANIA slice (the rooms module).\n422\t- The world is an `E.World` of PlatformMaps: each room its rows and `at: [gx, gy]` on a grid of screens; rooms meet where they touch, and an open edge is a door (leave walls elsewhere). After the hero moves, `world.update(dt, hero)` returns `'enter'` on the step it crosses into the next room: spawn that room's creatures then (from `world.map.spawns`); hold the game while `world.moving` (the slide).\n423\t- Abilities open the way: `world.give('boots')` then `hero.airJumps = world.has('boots') ? 1 : 0` (and `dash`); a gate is a tile type with `gate: 'dash'`, solid until given. The map: `world.drawMap(g, x, y, cell)` on a map screen and, clipped round `world.here`, in a corner. Saves: `world.save(hero)` at a station, `world.load()` on the next visit.\n424\t- An arm cannon aimed eight ways (the run-and-gun recipe): from `game.input.move()`, up aims up, up and forward the diagonal, down in the air (and forward) down or its diagonal, else forward; `rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ point: true, aim }))` with `aim` the angle above level (`Math.PI / 2` straight up); shots leave `rig.tip()` on `new E.Bullets(game, { plane: 'side' })`. Holding fire charges a bigger shot; a held button stands still to aim.\n425\t- Bosses are state machines: a telegraph (a glow, a warning line) before each attack, an opening when they can be hurt (armor turns shots otherwise); seal the room's doors while one lives and keep both in view with `game.cam.frame([hero, boss])`.\n426\t\n427\t**Zelda-like adventure.**\n428\t- Build rooms with `TileMap` rows and set `game.cam.room = [roomW, roomH]`.\n429\t- The hero is a `Body` moved by `game.view.screenDirToGround(...game.input.move())`. A sword swing is an `E.Attack` with `hits(foes, f => E.inArc(...), ...)`.\n430\t- Talk with `Dialog`, and open a locked door with `map.set(cx, cy, 0)` after checking a key count. Only run enemies in the hero's room, and freeze everything while `cam.moving`.\n431\t\n432\t**Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage).** Start from the BRAWLER slice.\n433\t- Use the `brawler` view with a long `TileMap` floor strip (walls along the top edge only) and `cam.bounds` that scroll right.\n434\t- Heroes and thugs are `Body` + `Humanoid` with `weapon: null`. Punches are attack specs with `blade: 0`; kicks use `kick: true, z0: 6, z1: 9`.\n435\t- Hits need `E.inArc` plus a small y difference (same lane). Knock down with `body.push(dx, dy, 120)` and let gravity drop them.\n436\t- A second player drops in (`game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin })`): a hero of its own driven by its input, foes going for the nearer hero, `cam.frame(heroes)` instead of `follow`, game over when both are out.\n437\t\n438\t**Fighting game (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers).** Start from the FIGHTING slice (the fight module).\n439\t- Each fighter is an `E.Fighter`: a roster entry's rig (a Humanoid sized about 2.2 to 2.7 at 320x240) and its moves, each an `E.MOVES` name with frame data in frames at 60 (`f: [startup, active, recovery]`), damage, hit- and block-stun, a guard (`'low'` sweeps, `'overhead'` jump-ins and axe kicks), `cancel: true` on normals a special may cut short, and specials by motion (`motion: '236'`, `'623'`, `'41236'`, `'236236'` for a super with `meter: 100`) or charge (`charge: '46'`). `shot` makes a projectile, `throw` a command grab, `teleport` a teleport, `vx` and `vz` a move that travels, `inv` invulnerable frames, `aa: true` an anti-air the CPU reaches for.\n440\t- `E.Match` runs the rounds, the clock, KO and time over, the calls and the HUD; `E.FightAI({ level, seed })` is a CPU whose matches repeat with its seed; `E.FightSelect` is the character select; `training: { dummy }` draws the boxes, the frame data and the inputs. Update the match once a step (motions are read at 120 Hz) and draw the stage before `match.draw(r)`.\n441\t- Two players: `game.allowJoin({ max: 2 })` and `inputs: [game.input, game.players[1]]` (a pad each, or half a keyboard each); the select and the menus read every player.\n442\t\n443\t**Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius).** Start from the SHOOTER slice: its first stage is vertical, its second horizontal.\n444\t- Use `view: 'overhead'` so world x/y equal screen pixels; `res: [240, 320]` for a vertical stage, `[320, 240]` for a horizontal one, with `expand`.\n445\t- Ships are sprites or vector parts drawn by code; a timeline array spawns waves by time or by how far the camera has come; enemies move on formulas (sine lines, dives aimed with `E.pattern.aim`) or along `E.Path`s.\n446\t- Flyers keep up with the camera: each step add the scroll to their position (and to the bullets'), while the ground and the rock stay put.\n447\t- Horizontal stages (Gradius, R-Type): the rock above and below is two functions of x (`highY(x)`, `lowY(x)`) drawn in columns and tested for crashes; a power bar is a cursor that capsules move along, a button taking the lit slot; options follow the ship's past path (a ring of its positions, an option every 12 behind); a mid-boss loops a closed `E.Path` while the scroll waits.\n448\t- Bosses fire `E.pattern.ring` and `spread`, each attack telegraphed. A bomb clears `shots.clear('foe')`. The end: `E.Arcade`'s continue, then initials and the table (the arcade module).\n449\t\n450\t**Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids).** Start from the ARCADE slice: a cabinet of three.\n451\t- One fixed screen (`res: [232, 270]` or `[320, 240]` with `expand`): centre the playfield and fill the rest with cabinet art drawn by code, or let the field be the whole screen (the rocks wrap at its edges).\n452\t- `new E.Arcade(game, { id, lives: 3, extra: [10000] })` keeps the score, lives, two players' turns (each one's stage in `p.save`), the continue, the tables with initials and the attract mode; `cab.update(dt)` each step and `cab.draw(r, box)` after the game.\n453\t- A maze is an `E.Grid` with `E.GridMover`s: the player's turn asked early is kept (`{ keep: true }`); a chaser picks, at each cell, the open way nearest its own target (never straight back). Moths fly in on `E.Path`s and turn with `r.sprite`'s `rot`; rocks drift with `E.wrap` and draw at every edge they cross.\n454\t- Each game's demo is its own bot, playing the real rules; a remaster (light, rigs, glow) with the classic look one key and one setting away.\n455\t\n456\t**Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero).** Start from the KART slice (the mode7 and drive modules).\n457\t- The track is a TileMap: its walls bounce the karts, its floor tags (`grass`, `sand`, `boost`, ...) slow or boost them (`E.Car`'s `surfaces`), and `E.Mode7({ floor: (x, y) => [r, g, b] })` draws it from the same cells. The racing line is a closed `E.Path` through the track's middle: `E.Race({ path, laps: 3 })` counts laps through its checkpoints in order (a shortcut counts nothing), gives places, and drives every car not a player's (`race.add(car, { input })` for a player, `{ ai: { skill } }` for the rest).\n458\t- Each step `race.update(dt, map)` (it moves every car), then the camera behind yours, turning after it more slowly than the kart turns (a drift shows): `m7.cam = cam; m7.chase({ x, y, angle: camAngle }, { back: 42, h: 24 })`. Draw the karts with `m7.add(x, y, z, (g, sx, sy, k) => ...)`: a few boxes through `m7.view(k)`'s projection and the rig seated (`down: .4`) in the middle, sorted far to near from the camera's side.\n459\t- Two players: `game.allowJoin({ max: 2 })`, `game.splitScreen(2, { layout: 'rows' })`, and in `draw` each viewport its own camera: `m7.draw(r, cams[r.viewport.i])`, `race.hud(r, players[r.viewport.i])`. On a phone the gas goes on by itself (`car.o.auto`), the stick steers, DRIFT and ITEM are buttons; held upright, draw from further back with a wider field of view.\n460\t\n461\t**Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Hang-On).** Start from the ROAD slice (the road module).\n462\t- `E.Road` is the course: `road.add(n, { curve, hill, tunnel, colors })` stretch by stretch; scenery with `road.place(z, offset, sprite, scale)` (`E.prop('palm').frames[0]` works), traffic as `cars: [{ z, x, w, draw }]` passed to `road.draw`. The player is a position (`z` along, `x` across in half roads) and a speed, not a body.\n463\t- Each step: `x -= road.curveAt(z) * speed² * k` throws the car outward on a bend (make the hardest faster than the player can steer: they slow down), off the road (`|x| > 1`) it slows, `road.hits(z, x, w)` crashes it into the scenery (put it back on the road), and a car close in `z` and `x` bumps it. A clock that counts down and checkpoints that add time make the race.\n464\t\n465\t**Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA).** `E.Car` on a TileMap in the `topdown` view: the same cars, laps and drivers as the kart (`E.Race`), drawn from above (a box of `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points, turned by `car.angle`); GTA's car is a `E.Car` the hero gets into (`kart: false`: drift is a handbrake).\n466\t\n467\t**JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy).** Start from the RPG slice (the story and rpg modules).\n468\t- The field is a scene of `TileMap`s: a town, a world map (past 128 x 128 cells its floor bakes in chunks: paint woods and peaks into `floorTex`, block them), dungeons; a map's floor tag leads to another (an exit, a cave's mouth). Walk a `Body` with the stick; the other members follow the leader's trail.\n469\t- People talk through an `E.Story`: their lines as data, with choices, flags, quests, emote balloons and events for the game's scripts (a shop's window, the inn's night, a boss's fight; `wait: true` holds the talk until `story.resume()`).\n470\t- The party is an `E.RPG` party: members with stats, levels and gear, a bag, gold; shops, items and `reward(troop)` after a fight. Encounters by zone (`rpg.encounters`) as the party walks: a swirl (`game.go(battle, data, { fade: 'swirl', at })`) into the battle scene, which reads the members' `stat()` and writes their hp back.\n471\t- Battles are a scene in the `brawler` view with rigs lined up, a `Menu` of commands and a queue of actions (the RPG slice's ATB battle). Each action steps forward, swings, applies damage and steps back.\n472\t- Saves: `game.saves` (the party's `state()`, the story's `state()`, the map and the place); a crystal to save at, the inn's autosave, Continue and Load on the title. The menu's windows are `rpg.list` and `rpg.card`, rows a thumb's height on a phone.\n473\t\n474\t**Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo Puyo).** Start from the PUZZLE slice.\n475\t- The field is an array of cells drawn in screen pixels (`r.overlay`) over a painted stage; every rule is a small function: spawn, fits, kicks (SRS), lock (half a second, reset by a move up to 15 times), clear, gravity (the guideline's curve by level). A bag of seven from `E.rng`, a next queue, hold, a ghost.\n476\t- `input.repeat('left', .17, .05)` gives the delayed auto-shift. Touch: `input.steer = 'drag'` and `input.drag()` move a cell a step of the thumb, `input.clicked()` (a tap) turns, a quick flick drops or holds.\n477\t- Versus: one field's state swapped in and out (save and load its variables) to step and draw two side by side from one seed, each read through its own player's input; two lines or more cleared send rows to the other, rising at its next piece.\n478\t- Matching games (Puyo, Columns, match-3): `new E.Grid(w, h).groups((x, y) => field[y * w + x] || null)` gives the groups of one colour; pop those of four or more, let what is above fall, and count each round that pops as a chain.\n479\t\n480\t**Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren).** Start from the ROGUELIKE slice.\n481\t- A floor is `E.gen.rooms(46, 30, { seed })` (or `bsp`, `cave`), its spawns put in with `E.gen.mark(level, 'g', 5)` and read by a `TileMap` legend; `E.Grid.from(map, { diagonal: true })` gives `path`, `reach` and `fov`, and `E.Fog` covers what isn't in sight (`fog.draw(r, 16, { height })` over the walls too).\n482\t- `E.Turns([hero, ...foes], { mode: 'speed' })`: the hero acts on a key, then `next()` runs every monster's turn until the hero's comes round again (speed 200 acts twice); a monster walks its next step of `grid.path` toward the hero, or strikes when it is next to it.\n483\t- The game is turn-based but its pictures are not: each step is `game.tween(actor, { x, y }, .13)` and the rigs walk through it. The stairs are `game.go(scene, { depth }, { fade: 'iris', at: hero })`. A bot (`bot()` in the slice) that plays to the end is the best test: pathing, fighting and items all exercised.\n484\t\n485\t**Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem).** Start from the TACTICS slice (the grid and story modules).\n486\t- The map is a `TileMap` whose cells are all blocks at their heights (a base of 16 so the earth shows at its edges; a level is `E.HEIGHTS.level`, 8), in the `iso` view turned a quarter at a time (`game.rotateView(90)`) and zoomed (`camera: { zoom }`). `E.Grid.from(map, { tops: true, cost })` stands units on the tops (shallows `cost` 2)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67077d04-87f0-4158-a4f5-843d0a0e9941","uuid":"39ffd30a-25d7-4f75-9dc6-f0ad37314aa1","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:49:42.814Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.cache/x2e02c0-plan-research/engine/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md","content":"- `E.ui.box/bar/hearts` draw classic windows and meters inside `r.overlay`.\n- `Dialog` does typewriter text with blips, pages, speaker names and choices; `update` returns true while it is open, so the world waits. Its markup gives a word its color (`{c=#ffd36a}gold{/c}`), a beat before the next line (`{w=.5}`), a slow dread (`{s=12}...{/s}`), a scream that shakes (`{shake}`) or a song that bobs (`{wave}`), and the hero's name (`{$name}` from `vars`).\n- `Menu` handles up and down with key repeat, confirm, cancel and disabled items.\n\n### Shell (an HTML interface: screens and HUD)\nA modern adaptation (Assassin's Creed, Breath of the Wild) wants readable HTML screens over the pixel world, not pixel windows. `E.Shell` builds them from data, so you write none of the HTML, CSS or focus handling. It shows the game's state and holds none of it: you pass values in each step and callbacks for the buttons. The STEALTH slice (`src/starter/70-stealth.js`: the objective, health, status, the takedown prompt, toasts, the results) and the template game use it; copy from them.\n- `const ui = new E.Shell(game, { theme: { ink, paper, accent, muted, danger, line, font, titleFont }, settings: { items: [{ key: 'cones', label: 'Sight cones', value: true }], onChange(s, key) {} }, screens: { pause: {...}, help: {...} } })`. It takes over from the engine's own and keeps the standard menu (see Standard features). Your items join `game.settings` (`ui.settings` is the same object), saved and checked with the rest; `onChange` hears every change.\n- Screens: `ui.show(name, spec)`, `ui.back()`, `ui.hide()`, `ui.screen` (the open one's name, or null). Every spec takes `kicker`, `title`, `text` (strings or functions), `actions: [[label, fn], { label, act, primary, disabled }]` (falsy entries are skipped, so `cond && [...]` works; `act` taps an input action) and `back`. Then: `'title'` (`name`, `subtitle`, `meta`, `footer`), `'pause'` (Resume first, then your actions, then Settings, Controls, Full screen, Help and Quit to title; it opens while `game.paused` and Resume unpauses; `screens: { pause: false }` leaves it out), `'settings'`, `'controls'`, `'help'` (`keys: [[key, what]]`, `sections: [[heading, text]]`), `'results'` (`emblem`, `stats: [[value, label]]`, `medals: [[label, earned]]`) and `'map'` (`map`, `at`, `markers`, `labels`, `onPick(x, y)` for a waypoint). A screen opened from another stacks; cancel goes back.\n- The HUD, each step: `ui.hud({ objective: { step, title, text, at, from }, status, bars: [{ label, value, max, kind: 'pips' | 'bar', color }], counts: [[label, value]], prompt: { key, label, hint, progress }, tip, place, keys, buttons: [[label, fn]], minimap: { map, at, markers, onClick } })`. A piece left out is hidden, only what changed is redrawn, and the HUD hides while a screen is open. `objective` shows the distance in meters (`meters: .125` a world unit) and whether the target is above or below. `ui.toast(title, text, seconds)` and `ui.tutorial(title, text, seconds)` come and go on their own.\n- Input: the game's own input moves the focus (keys, d-pad, stick: up / down / left / right, a slider or a choice turns left and right), confirm presses, cancel goes back, pause closes the menu; touch taps. While a screen is open the game's actions are held back, so a menu press doesn't also attack. Touch players reach the menu with the touch START or the corner's button; `buttons: [['Map', openMap]]` adds your own.\n- It honors reduced motion and the safe-area insets, is headless-safe (it keeps its screens' names and draws nothing), and `game.dispose()` takes it away.\n\n### Lighting\n- Light every scene (the Look target's step 9): a dark game with no lights reads flat, and a judge says so. Canvas lighting leads because it draws the same in every browser and every headless check: set `lights.enabled` and `ambient` (.4-.6 for dusk and interiors) in the scene's `enter`, then call `lights.add(x, y, z, radius, intensity, { color })` every frame where light would come from (lamps, torches, windows, the objective's glow). Light is measured on the ground in ground views, and on screen in side views and for PlatformMaps. The template game and the STEALTH slice show it. Its pass costs a few milliseconds a frame at 320x240 (more on a phone): a heavy scene can keep it for a computer, `matchMedia('(pointer: fine)').matches`, as the STEALTH slice does.\n- GPU lighting (`game.enableGPU({ map })`) needs WebGPU and a TileMap. It adds:\n  - colored lights, and soft shadows cast by walls and `lights.caster(...)`;\n  - light that wraps around characters;\n  - glow from `px.glow`, emissive decals and actors, and `[r, g, b, glow]` floor texels;\n  - bloom and heat shimmer (`lights.heat`).\n- Where GPU lighting can't draw (no WebGPU, still loading, a side view) the canvas lighting stands in, with `lights.ambient` and the same lights: set both ambients (`gpu.ambient = [r, g, b]`, `lights.ambient`) so the two look alike (the ADVENTURE slice does); `gpu.fallback = false` leaves such frames unlit. Tune the settings, not the shaders.\n\n### Errors and warnings\n- Runtime errors appear in a red box (click to hide) and in the console, labeled with `update` or `draw` and the scene.\n- Setup errors (before `game.start`) are shown too.\n- One-time `my-3D2dge:` warnings flag:\n  - unknown views, actions, sounds, songs, resolutions and input presets;\n  - legend characters, wall or tile types missing from `types`;\n  - uneven level rows and non-hex colors;\n  - lights with bad numbers, and PlatformMaps drawn in the wrong view.\n- The checker prints them all.\n\n### Testing without a browser\nA game runs in Node with no screen, so its rules can be tested in a second, exactly:\n- `new E.Game()` is headless where there is no DOM (or with `{ headless: true }`): no screen, renderer, sound or DOM input; scenes update and `draw` is skipped. Sprites made while the game loads get a stand-in canvas.\n- `game.advance(seconds)` runs exact fixed steps at once: `advance(1)` is 120 updates. In a page, `new E.Game({ loop: false })` waits for `advance` too, and `advance(s, { draw: true })` draws a frame after.\n- Input: `game.input.tap('jump')` presses an action for one step (`pressed` and `down` in the next step, `released` in the one after); `hold('right')` holds it until `hold('right', false)`; `press(code)` and `release(code)` press keys.\n- `game.debug = { snapshot() { return { scene, hero, score } }, teleport(x, y, z) {...} }`: your game's handle. Tests read it; `tools/check.mjs` prints it after play; `tools/replay.mjs` logs it at the end of a replay.\n- `game.stop()` stops the loop, `game.start()` resumes it, `game.dispose()` ends the game and removes every listener the engine added to the page.\n\n```js\n// game/test.mjs in the handoff package: load the engine and the game into one context, then drive it\nconst ctx = { console }; vm.createContext(ctx);\nfor (const f of ['../my-3d2dge.js', './game.js']) vm.runInContext(readFileSync(new URL(f, import.meta.url), 'utf8'), ctx);\nconst game = ctx.My3D2dge.current;\nconst I = game.input; I.tap('start'); game.advance(.5);\ngame.debug.teleport(100, 88, 0, 0); I.tap('jump'); game.advance(.1);   // facing the roof's wall: jump takes hold of it\nI.hold('up'); game.advance(.6); I.hold('up', false); game.advance(1);   // up the screen is up the wall: it pulls up at the top\nif (game.debug.snapshot().hero[2] !== 32) throw new Error('the hero should have climbed onto the roof');\n```\n\n### The handoff package\n`dist/handoff/` (on the site: `/handoff/`; `node tools/handoff.mjs --zip <file>` zips it for a chat) holds what a coding agent needs to build on the full engine without the repository: both editions, each module on its own (`modules/`), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, this guide, the tools that check, film and replay a game, the asset card with its tools and the store, a `package.json` pinning Playwright, `game/` (a template game with a headless test) and `kits/` (each 2D genre kit's game, readable, with the page that plays it and a headless test). Its `README.md` says where to start; `tools/build.mjs` writes it from `src/handoff/`. It leaves out the 3D engine and its kits (on the site and in the repository).\n\n### Modules (general systems in files of their own)\nA module (`engine/modules/<name>.js`) holds one general system that several genres share, outside the core, so that only the games that use it carry it. It loads after the engine, in a `<script>` of its own, and attaches its names to `My3D2dge`; its header is its manual (what it holds, its API, a complete example, its rules), and the index has a section for it. Its manual's third line says whether it also loads after the agent edition, and which modules it needs first.\n- **In a single file:** the reference editions (`dist/my-3d2dge.html`, the compact one) carry every module; a kit carries the modules its slice names in its header (`@modules grid gen`). To use one a kit hasn't got, paste the module's file in a `<script>` after the engine's (if the console then names a section the kit's engine left out, start from the reference edition).\n- **With files:** `dist/my-3d2dge.js` (`my-3d2dge.js` in the handoff package) is the core and every module in one file; the handoff package also has each module on its own (`modules/<name>.js`), to load after the agent edition (the twelve that can).\n- **The modules:** `grid` (square and hex grids: A* paths, move ranges with climb and drop past allies, lines, symmetric fields of view, `E.Fog`, flood fills and groups, tactics' shapes of effect, facing and threat maps, `E.GridMover` for Pac-Man's and Sokoban's moves, `E.Turns` by speed, charge time, phase or ATB), `gen` (floors from a seed: rooms and corridors, BSP, caves, mazes, Poisson scatter, as ASCII rows a `TileMap` reads, with `E.gen.mark` for spawns), `rooms` (a world of rooms on a grid of screens: doors at open edges, a slide or a fade between them, the explored map, gates and saves: `E.World`), `arcade` (the coin-op's frame: a score that floats up where it was won, extra lives at thresholds, two players taking turns, credits and a continue, high-score tables with initials on a letter wheel, stage cards, the attract mode), `mode7` (Super Mario Kart's and F-Zero's view: a camera over a flat floor drawn a row at a time, the sky turning with it, billboards scaled by distance, `E.Mode7`) and `road` (OutRun's road: segments with curves, hills and tunnels, scenery and cars scaled by distance, `E.Road`) and `drive` (cars with grip, drift and the ground's tag, races with laps, checkpoints, drivers and items: `E.Car`, `E.Race`), `story` (dialogue as data: lines, choices with conditions, flags, quests, events, emote balloons: `E.Story`), `rpg` (items, bags, members with levels and gear, status, a party, shops, damage, encounters, windows: `E.RPG`), `physics` (2D rigid bodies: circles, boxes, polygons, chains; flippers, bumpers, kickers: `E.Physics`), `terrain` (destructible ground in the side plane: carve, walk, ray: `E.Terrain`) and `songs` (the songbook: `E.Songbook`): these twelve load after the agent edition too (a race's `E.Path` is the full engine's). Four load after the full engine only: `fight` (one-on-one fighting: `E.Fighter`, `E.Match`, `E.FightAI`, `E.FightSelect`, training), `click` (point-and-click: `E.Click`, `E.Walkbox`), `rhythm` (`E.Rhythm`: charts, judgments, holds, calls) and `strategy` (`E.Strategy`, `E.Clock`, `E.needs`; it needs `grid`).\n- **Writing one:** `engine/modules/README.md` (the header's form, the rules, its budget of 1,500 tokens; `tools/modules-test.mjs` proves the path with a fixture). `docs/KITS.md` says which systems become modules, and what every kit has.\n\n### Kits and the diet (which file for which game)\nA genre kit (`dist/kits/my-3d2dge-<genre>.html`) is the engine, the API card and one slice, sized for a chat model's context: a 2D kit is at most 200,000 tokens, six of them up to about 241,000 (`doctrine-check` counts them). The handoff package's `kits/` holds each 2D kit's game readable, on the full engine. Its engine is on the **kit diet**: the core less the sections its game never calls (the tile map in the platformer's kit, the third-person kit and GPU lighting in most), as the kit's header lists. Calling a left-out name warns once in the console, naming its section (a constructor throws). So:\n- **A game in the kit's genre:** start from the kit; anything its slice uses is there.\n- **A game of several genres** (a platformer with a top-down overworld, an RPG with a shoot-'em-up stage), or one that needs a section a kit left out: start from `dist/my-3d2dge.html` (every slice and section, readable) or `dist/my-3d2dge-compact.html` (the same, minified), or `dist/my-3d2dge.js` with your own page.\n- **The presentation sections** (Penner's easings beyond the core's, sprite frames and turns, tweens and scripts, paths, transitions, weather, input sequences, the camera's window and framing) are each a part of the diet: a kit carries the ones its slice uses, and a kit whose slice uses none carries about 1,600 tokens of them in its engine (the screen filter, a standard setting, and the small ways in) and 1,000 in its card.\n- **Its proof:** each slice has `run(n)` (`__game.scenes.<id>.run(600)`): n fixed steps of scripted play from a seed, returning a hash that is the same every time; `node kits/test.mjs` in the handoff package runs each kit's twice, headless (the repository's `tools/kits-test.mjs`, in a browser). A game copied from a slice can keep one: the shell's `proof()` is how.\n\n### Recorded sessions (a bug from a phone, replayed exactly)\n- `E.session.record(true)` turns recording on for every later page load (`?record=1` does it for one load). It starts before the game's code and keeps everything a game's next frame depends on:\n  - a seed for `Math.random`;\n  - each display frame's time (`performance.now` and `Date.now` read it during that frame);\n  - every input and the frame it arrived before: keys, pointer and touch, wheel, clicks and form edits, focus, visibility, resize, full screen;\n  - gamepad readings, the media queries the game asked, the safe-area insets, the screen, and the save (`localStorage`) at the start;\n  - a checksum every 60 frames.\n- `E.session.share()` saves the session: the share sheet on a phone, a download elsewhere. In Emberdeep it is Settings → Tools → Record a session; on any recorded page the standard pause menu has Save session (a phone has no console). `node tools/replay.mjs <file> --pacing` reads the device's refreshes from it without replaying: the refresh, the ones missed second by second, how unevenly (a report of choppy play).\n- `node tools/replay.mjs session.json` replays it in headless Chromium at the recorded screen size, frame by frame, and reports the first checksum that differs. Useful options:\n  - `--to 1800` stops at a frame; `--shots 600,1200` saves screenshots;\n  - `--log \"My3D2dge.current.sceneName\"` prints any state at the end (a game's `game.debug.snapshot()` prints by itself);\n  - `--page` replays on another page. Fix the bug, rebuild, and replay the same session to see it gone.\n- `game.stateHash = () => [hero.x, hero.y, hero.hp, foes.length]` adds a game's own state to the checksum, so a replay stops at the first frame where that state differs.\n- Why it works: the clock turns the same frame times into the same fixed steps, so the same frame times, inputs and seed give the same game.\n- Any browser: while recording and replaying, `Math.sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `atan`, `atan2`, `asin`, `acos`, `exp`, `log`, `log2`, `log10`, `pow`, `hypot` and `cbrt` are the engine's portable versions (`E.session.math`).\n  - Browsers may round these differently in the last bit: an iPhone's Safari and Chromium use different libraries.\n  - The portable versions are built only from `+ - * /` and `sqrt`, which are exact everywhere.\n  - So a session recorded in one browser replays in another bit for bit.\n  - `sin`, `cos`, `atan`, `atan2`, `exp` and `log` are fdlibm's algorithms, the same bits as Chromium's own.\n  - The others are within a few units in the last place.\n  - `node tools/cross-engine-math.cjs` checks this in Safari's own engine. It runs the same code in V8 and in JavaScriptCore, through Bun (`BUN=/path/to/bun`).\n  - Native math gives different bits in the two engines, and so does a Humanoid animated with it.\n  - The portable math, and the same Humanoid with it, give the same bits in both.\n- Limits:\n  - the GPU lighting is off while recording, because it starts up on real time;\n  - a session is one page load;\n  - replay on the build it was recorded on: its version and build are in the file.\n\n## Genre recipes\nEach recipe names the kit to start from (`kits/<id>.js` in the handoff package). The 3D ones build on the 3D engine, whose kits are on the site and in the repository, not in the handoff package.\n\n**Side-scroller (Mario, Mega Man, Castlevania).**\n```js\nconst level = new E.PlatformMap({ rows: LEVEL, legend: { '#': 1, '=': 2, '?': 3, '@': 'hero', 'g': 'walker' },\n  types: { 1: { style: 'ground', side: '#8a5a32' }, 2: { kind: 'oneway' }, 3: { style: 'bonus', side: '#e8a830' } } });\nconst s = level.find('hero'), hero = new E.Platformer({ x: s.x, z: s.z }), rig = new E.Humanoid({ weapon: 'gun', hat: 'cap' });\n// scene: { view: 'side', views: ['side', 'brawler'], input: 'PLATFORMER', res: 'snes', pausable: true, ... }\n// update: hero.update(dt, level, game.input, lifts); if (hero.jumped) game.audio.sfx('jump');\n//         if (hero.bumped) { ...change level.set(cx, cz, id)... }\n//         rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ point: game.input.down('attack') })); game.focus(hero.x, 0, hero.z + 24);\n// draw:   r.sky([...]); level.draw(r); r.actor(hero.x, 0, hero.z, (g, ox, oy) => rig.draw(g, ox, oy, r.view));\n```\n- Enemies are boxes moved by `level.move(e, dt)` with your own gravity: turn at `hitWall`, and at ledges using `groundBelow`.\n- Stomp when `hero.vz < 0` and the hero's feet are above the enemy's middle.\n- Pits: `hero.z < -40`. Spikes: `level.touching(hero, 'hazard')`.\n- Further moves, on request: `new E.Platformer({ crouch: true, ledge: true, wallCling: true, momentum: true })`; tiles of `kind: 'water' | 'rope' | 'bars'`, and grip on any tile type: `friction` (ice: .15), `carry` (a conveyor's speed), `bounce` (a spring), `crumble` with `respawn` (call `level.update(dt)` each step); `hero.swingFrom(x, z)` hangs it from a grapple point. Draw `level.drawWater(r)` after the actors; `rigState()` poses every move (hang, climb, crawl, swim, swing).\n\n**Metroidvania (Metroid, Super Metroid, Symphony of the Night).** Start from the METROIDVANIA slice (the rooms module).\n- The world is an `E.World` of PlatformMaps: each room its rows and `at: [gx, gy]` on a grid of screens; rooms meet where they touch, and an open edge is a door (leave walls elsewhere). After the hero moves, `world.update(dt, hero)` returns `'enter'` on the step it crosses into the next room: spawn that room's creatures then (from `world.map.spawns`); hold the game while `world.moving` (the slide).\n- Abilities open the way: `world.give('boots')` then `hero.airJumps = world.has('boots') ? 1 : 0` (and `dash`); a gate is a tile type with `gate: 'dash'`, solid until given. The map: `world.drawMap(g, x, y, cell)` on a map screen and, clipped round `world.here`, in a corner. Saves: `world.save(hero)` at a station, `world.load()` on the next visit.\n- An arm cannon aimed eight ways (the run-and-gun recipe): from `game.input.move()`, up aims up, up and forward the diagonal, down in the air (and forward) down or its diagonal, else forward; `rig.update(dt, hero.rigState({ point: true, aim }))` with `aim` the angle above level (`Math.PI / 2` straight up); shots leave `rig.tip()` on `new E.Bullets(game, { plane: 'side' })`. Holding fire charges a bigger shot; a held button stands still to aim.\n- Bosses are state machines: a telegraph (a glow, a warning line) before each attack, an opening when they can be hurt (armor turns shots otherwise); seal the room's doors while one lives and keep both in view with `game.cam.frame([hero, boss])`.\n\n**Zelda-like adventure.**\n- Build rooms with `TileMap` rows and set `game.cam.room = [roomW, roomH]`.\n- The hero is a `Body` moved by `game.view.screenDirToGround(...game.input.move())`. A sword swing is an `E.Attack` with `hits(foes, f => E.inArc(...), ...)`.\n- Talk with `Dialog`, and open a locked door with `map.set(cx, cy, 0)` after checking a key count. Only run enemies in the hero's room, and freeze everything while `cam.moving`.\n\n**Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage).** Start from the BRAWLER slice.\n- Use the `brawler` view with a long `TileMap` floor strip (walls along the top edge only) and `cam.bounds` that scroll right.\n- Heroes and thugs are `Body` + `Humanoid` with `weapon: null`. Punches are attack specs with `blade: 0`; kicks use `kick: true, z0: 6, z1: 9`.\n- Hits need `E.inArc` plus a small y difference (same lane). Knock down with `body.push(dx, dy, 120)` and let gravity drop them.\n- A second player drops in (`game.allowJoin({ max: 2, onJoin })`): a hero of its own driven by its input, foes going for the nearer hero, `cam.frame(heroes)` instead of `follow`, game over when both are out.\n\n**Fighting game (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers).** Start from the FIGHTING slice (the fight module).\n- Each fighter is an `E.Fighter`: a roster entry's rig (a Humanoid sized about 2.2 to 2.7 at 320x240) and its moves, each an `E.MOVES` name with frame data in frames at 60 (`f: [startup, active, recovery]`), damage, hit- and block-stun, a guard (`'low'` sweeps, `'overhead'` jump-ins and axe kicks), `cancel: true` on normals a special may cut short, and specials by motion (`motion: '236'`, `'623'`, `'41236'`, `'236236'` for a super with `meter: 100`) or charge (`charge: '46'`). `shot` makes a projectile, `throw` a command grab, `teleport` a teleport, `vx` and `vz` a move that travels, `inv` invulnerable frames, `aa: true` an anti-air the CPU reaches for.\n- `E.Match` runs the rounds, the clock, KO and time over, the calls and the HUD; `E.FightAI({ level, seed })` is a CPU whose matches repeat with its seed; `E.FightSelect` is the character select; `training: { dummy }` draws the boxes, the frame data and the inputs. Update the match once a step (motions are read at 120 Hz) and draw the stage before `match.draw(r)`.\n- Two players: `game.allowJoin({ max: 2 })` and `inputs: [game.input, game.players[1]]` (a pad each, or half a keyboard each); the select and the menus read every player.\n\n**Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius).** Start from the SHOOTER slice: its first stage is vertical, its second horizontal.\n- Use `view: 'overhead'` so world x/y equal screen pixels; `res: [240, 320]` for a vertical stage, `[320, 240]` for a horizontal one, with `expand`.\n- Ships are sprites or vector parts drawn by code; a timeline array spawns waves by time or by how far the camera has come; enemies move on formulas (sine lines, dives aimed with `E.pattern.aim`) or along `E.Path`s.\n- Flyers keep up with the camera: each step add the scroll to their position (and to the bullets'), while the ground and the rock stay put.\n- Horizontal stages (Gradius, R-Type): the rock above and below is two functions of x (`highY(x)`, `lowY(x)`) drawn in columns and tested for crashes; a power bar is a cursor that capsules move along, a button taking the lit slot; options follow the ship's past path (a ring of its positions, an option every 12 behind); a mid-boss loops a closed `E.Path` while the scroll waits.\n- Bosses fire `E.pattern.ring` and `spread`, each attack telegraphed. A bomb clears `shots.clear('foe')`. The end: `E.Arcade`'s continue, then initials and the table (the arcade module).\n\n**Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids).** Start from the ARCADE slice: a cabinet of three.\n- One fixed screen (`res: [232, 270]` or `[320, 240]` with `expand`): centre the playfield and fill the rest with cabinet art drawn by code, or let the field be the whole screen (the rocks wrap at its edges).\n- `new E.Arcade(game, { id, lives: 3, extra: [10000] })` keeps the score, lives, two players' turns (each one's stage in `p.save`), the continue, the tables with initials and the attract mode; `cab.update(dt)` each step and `cab.draw(r, box)` after the game.\n- A maze is an `E.Grid` with `E.GridMover`s: the player's turn asked early is kept (`{ keep: true }`); a chaser picks, at each cell, the open way nearest its own target (never straight back). Moths fly in on `E.Path`s and turn with `r.sprite`'s `rot`; rocks drift with `E.wrap` and draw at every edge they cross.\n- Each game's demo is its own bot, playing the real rules; a remaster (light, rigs, glow) with the classic look one key and one setting away.\n\n**Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero).** Start from the KART slice (the mode7 and drive modules).\n- The track is a TileMap: its walls bounce the karts, its floor tags (`grass`, `sand`, `boost`, ...) slow or boost them (`E.Car`'s `surfaces`), and `E.Mode7({ floor: (x, y) => [r, g, b] })` draws it from the same cells. The racing line is a closed `E.Path` through the track's middle: `E.Race({ path, laps: 3 })` counts laps through its checkpoints in order (a shortcut counts nothing), gives places, and drives every car not a player's (`race.add(car, { input })` for a player, `{ ai: { skill } }` for the rest).\n- Each step `race.update(dt, map)` (it moves every car), then the camera behind yours, turning after it more slowly than the kart turns (a drift shows): `m7.cam = cam; m7.chase({ x, y, angle: camAngle }, { back: 42, h: 24 })`. Draw the karts with `m7.add(x, y, z, (g, sx, sy, k) => ...)`: a few boxes through `m7.view(k)`'s projection and the rig seated (`down: .4`) in the middle, sorted far to near from the camera's side.\n- Two players: `game.allowJoin({ max: 2 })`, `game.splitScreen(2, { layout: 'rows' })`, and in `draw` each viewport its own camera: `m7.draw(r, cams[r.viewport.i])`, `race.hud(r, players[r.viewport.i])`. On a phone the gas goes on by itself (`car.o.auto`), the stick steers, DRIFT and ITEM are buttons; held upright, draw from further back with a wider field of view.\n\n**Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Hang-On).** Start from the ROAD slice (the road module).\n- `E.Road` is the course: `road.add(n, { curve, hill, tunnel, colors })` stretch by stretch; scenery with `road.place(z, offset, sprite, scale)` (`E.prop('palm').frames[0]` works), traffic as `cars: [{ z, x, w, draw }]` passed to `road.draw`. The player is a position (`z` along, `x` across in half roads) and a speed, not a body.\n- Each step: `x -= road.curveAt(z) * speed² * k` throws the car outward on a bend (make the hardest faster than the player can steer: they slow down), off the road (`|x| > 1`) it slows, `road.hits(z, x, w)` crashes it into the scenery (put it back on the road), and a car close in `z` and `x` bumps it. A clock that counts down and checkpoints that add time make the race.\n\n**Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA).** `E.Car` on a TileMap in the `topdown` view: the same cars, laps and drivers as the kart (`E.Race`), drawn from above (a box of `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points, turned by `car.angle`); GTA's car is a `E.Car` the hero gets into (`kart: false`: drift is a handbrake).\n\n**JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy).** Start from the RPG slice (the story and rpg modules).\n- The field is a scene of `TileMap`s: a town, a world map (past 128 x 128 cells its floor bakes in chunks: paint woods and peaks into `floorTex`, block them), dungeons; a map's floor tag leads to another (an exit, a cave's mouth). Walk a `Body` with the stick; the other members follow the leader's trail.\n- People talk through an `E.Story`: their lines as data, with choices, flags, quests, emote balloons and events for the game's scripts (a shop's window, the inn's night, a boss's fight; `wait: true` holds the talk until `story.resume()`).\n- The party is an `E.RPG` party: members with stats, levels and gear, a bag, gold; shops, items and `reward(troop)` after a fight. Encounters by zone (`rpg.encounters`) as the party walks: a swirl (`game.go(battle, data, { fade: 'swirl', at })`) into the battle scene, which reads the members' `stat()` and writes their hp back.\n- Battles are a scene in the `brawler` view with rigs lined up, a `Menu` of commands and a queue of actions (the RPG slice's ATB battle). Each action steps forward, swings, applies damage and steps back.\n- Saves: `game.saves` (the party's `state()`, the story's `state()`, the map and the place); a crystal to save at, the inn's autosave, Continue and Load on the title. The menu's windows are `rpg.list` and `rpg.card`, rows a thumb's height on a phone.\n\n**Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo Puyo).** Start from the PUZZLE slice.\n- The field is an array of cells drawn in screen pixels (`r.overlay`) over a painted stage; every rule is a small function: spawn, fits, kicks (SRS), lock (half a second, reset by a move up to 15 times), clear, gravity (the guideline's curve by level). A bag of seven from `E.rng`, a next queue, hold, a ghost.\n- `input.repeat('left', .17, .05)` gives the delayed auto-shift. Touch: `input.steer = 'drag'` and `input.drag()` move a cell a step of the thumb, `input.clicked()` (a tap) turns, a quick flick drops or holds.\n- Versus: one field's state swapped in and out (save and load its variables) to step and draw two side by side from one seed, each read through its own player's input; two lines or more cleared send rows to the other, rising at its next piece.\n- Matching games (Puyo, Columns, match-3): `new E.Grid(w, h).groups((x, y) => field[y * w + x] || null)` gives the groups of one colour; pop those of four or more, let what is above fall, and count each round that pops as a chain.\n\n**Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren).** Start from the ROGUELIKE slice.\n- A floor is `E.gen.rooms(46, 30, { seed })` (or `bsp`, `cave`), its spawns put in with `E.gen.mark(level, 'g', 5)` and read by a `TileMap` legend; `E.Grid.from(map, { diagonal: true })` gives `path`, `reach` and `fov`, and `E.Fog` covers what isn't in sight (`fog.draw(r, 16, { height })` over the walls too).\n- `E.Turns([hero, ...foes], { mode: 'speed' })`: the hero acts on a key, then `next()` runs every monster's turn until the hero's comes round again (speed 200 acts twice); a monster walks its next step of `grid.path` toward the hero, or strikes when it is next to it.\n- The game is turn-based but its pictures are not: each step is `game.tween(actor, { x, y }, .13)` and the rigs walk through it. The stairs are `game.go(scene, { depth }, { fade: 'iris', at: hero })`. A bot (`bot()` in the slice) that plays to the end is the best test: pathing, fighting and items all exercised.\n\n**Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem).** Start from the TACTICS slice (the grid and story modules).\n- The map is a `TileMap` whose cells are all blocks at their heights (a base of 16 so the earth shows at its edges; a level is `E.HEIGHTS.level`, 8), in the `iso` view turned a quarter at a time (`game.rotateView(90)`) and zoomed (`camera: { zoom }`). `E.Grid.from(map, { tops: true, cost })` stands units on the tops (shallows `cost` 2).","numLines":180,"startLine":307,"totalLines":665}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WmsQCF8LJwe3uL54Dpspz2","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
