{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh1wwJnJQhCVuXrjMYMt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VXd85haawHhzcnyVAU94Lv","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174813__x2e02c0/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":140},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1190,"cache_read_input_tokens":414660,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1190},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67077d04-87f0-4158-a4f5-843d0a0e9941","uuid":"c4ec6570-96e2-4b24-b1bc-35a5c795bc28","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:26:07.695Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh1ww4e8yqU4eSTfZBrW","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01VXd85haawHhzcnyVAU94Lv":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174813__x2e02c0/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":140}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VXd85haawHhzcnyVAU94Lv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Big Buck Hunt-Off\n2\t\n3\tBuild plan for the developers who will make this game from `TASK.md` and this file alone. Read `TASK.md` first, then this\n4\tplan top to bottom once; after that, use it as a reference. Everything here is a decision unless it says \"optional\".\n5\tWhere a number is given, it is the starting default (a dev slider where marked), to be tuned in play.\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## 0. At a glance\n10\t\n11\t| | |\n12\t|---|---|\n13\t| **Title** | **Big Buck Hunt-Off** (on screen: `BIG BUCK HUNT-OFF`). Picked as TASK.md asks: 50 riffs on \"Big Buck Hunter Pro\" brainstormed, one drawn with Node's `crypto.randomInt` (it drew #33 of 50). |\n14\t| **Dev subfolder** | `BigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174813/` (GameTitle and SOURCE in PascalCase with spaces and punctuation dropped) |\n15\t| **Deliverable** | `BigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174813.html` in this folder's root (beside `PLAN.md`), plus `exit-interview.json` beside it |\n16\t| **Genre** | Arcade light-gun hunting shooter (a shooting gallery in a living landscape), with party versus and bonus mini-games |\n17\t| **Perspective** | First person, on rails: a fixed vantage per site with scripted camera pans over a pseudo-3D heightfield landscape. Plus a slow-motion bullet cam on trophy kills, side-on x-ray Shot Chart cards in the tallies, and 2D screens for menus and the Lodge |\n18\t| **Players** | 1 to 4 on one screen (simultaneous, competitive); 1 to 4 online in AI Hub sessions (ghost mode) |\n19\t| **Engine** | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff package (full engine `my-3d2dge.js`), extended with new modules |\n20\t\n21\t**Pitch.** It's the 41st Annual Big Buck Hunt-Off at Whisker Lake Lodge. You sit in a tree stand, a blind or a canoe and\n22\tlook out over a landscape that lives: deer, elk and moose wander, graze, stop and bolt across creek fords, cornfields,\n23\talpine meadows and frozen bogs. Pick out the bucks (only the bucks), pump your rifle between shots, and go for clean,\n24\tlong, running shots for big points. Shoot a doe and you sit out the rest of the site. After each site an x-ray Shot\n25\tChart shows exactly where every round went. Each hunt ends with a legendary trophy animal and a silly bonus round, and\n26\tyour total climbs the Hunt-Off standings against the lodge regulars. Friends can play on the same couch with their own\n27\treticles, or online through AI Hub.\n28\t\n29\t**Core loop (one sentence, put it at the top of the game code):** spot the bucks, shoot clean, pump, never shoot a doe;\n30\tbag them before they're gone, then read your shots in x-ray and climb the standings.\n31\t\n32\t**Session lengths:** Hunt-Off campaign about 17 minutes (3 hunts); a single hunt about 6 minutes; Party Quick Draw\n33\tabout 5 minutes; an AI Hub round 3 minutes; Practice Range about 3 minutes.\n34\t\n35\t---\n36\t\n37\t## 1. The source: what to capture, what not to copy\n38\t\n39\t### 1.1 What research confirmed about Big Buck Hunter Pro\n40\t\n41\tResearch done while planning: Wikipedia's Big Buck Hunter article and web search results (Giant Bomb, MobyGames and\n42\tretailer listings, quoted in search summaries; Giant Bomb and TV Tropes pages could not be fetched). Write \"unknown\"\n43\tin the reports for anything below that is not listed as confirmed.\n44\t\n45\t- Arcade, released January 2006; designed by Play Mechanix, produced and distributed by Raw Thrills. Confirmed.\n46\t- A two-gun cabinet with head-to-head play; the guns are pump-action (\"pump to reload\" is a speech call). Confirmed.\n47\t- Shoot the males (bucks, bulls) as they run across the screen; a player who shoots a female is out until the next\n48\t  site. Confirmed.\n49\t- A hunt is split into five sites called treks, and a bonus round ends the session; a round ends when every male is\n50\t  shot or some escape. Confirmed.\n51\t- Points depend on distance, the animal's weight and accuracy. Bucks go down to a head shot, a heart shot, or by being\n52\t  \"peppered\". Confirmed.\n53\t- About 5 huntable animals, 15 bonus rounds and 12 critters that appear alongside the main animals for bonus points\n54\t  (squirrels, rabbits, possums, skunks, raccoons, foxes, mountain lions, birds; sources disagree). Hunts are grouped by\n55\t  animal (\"Whitetail Adventure\", \"Elk Adventure\"). Monthly national tournaments. Confirmed in part.\n56\t- Tone: folksy, tongue-in-cheek rural humor with goofy bonus rounds and loud announcer calls. Confirmed in spirit\n57\t  (bonus round names found); exact announcer lines are unknown.\n58\t\n59\t### 1.2 Distinctive elements, turned into our mechanics\n60\t\n61\t| Source element | Our version (original) |\n62\t|---|---|\n63\t| Two pump guns, head to head | Up to 4 simultaneous reticles, each with its own pump-action rifle; shared animals, first hit takes it |\n64\t| Bucks only, a doe puts you out | Bucks/bulls are targets; does, fawns and calves are protected. Classic rule: shoot one and you're benched for the site (a Casual setting turns it into a points penalty) |\n65\t| Sites (treks) and a bonus round | 3 hunts, each with 3 sites, a legendary **Trophy** encounter and a bonus game, all on one landscape per hunt with walk-in camera moves between vantages |\n66\t| Distance, weight and accuracy scoring | Points = species base (from antler size) × distance × hit zone × running bonus, plus accuracy, clean sweep and critter bonuses |\n67\t| Head and heart shots | Real 3D hit zones on every animal (heart, lungs, head, neck, shoulder, gut, ham, legs, antlers), shown afterwards on an **x-ray Shot Chart** |\n68\t| Critters for bonus points | 12 critter designs across the hunts; some protected (skunk, Bessie the cow, eagle, loons) with comic penalties |\n69\t| Goofy bonus rounds | Crow Patrol, Saucer Season, Snowball Standoff (ours) |\n70\t| Tournaments | The Hunt-Off campaign against rival regulars; AI Hub rounds as an online Hunt-Off |\n71\t| Pump to reload | Pump (rack) after every shot; when the tube is empty, the pump reloads it |\n72\t| Loud announcer | An emcee with short all-caps barks, plus a folksy guide and rivals who talk in Animalese gibberish with captions |\n73\t\n74\t### 1.3 Additions that make it a better game\n75\t- **The call** (grunt, bugle, moose grunt): a limited button that makes nearby animals stop and look for a moment: a\n76\t  skill shot setup, and the key to two trophies.\n77\t- **The scope**: a round magnifying lens around your reticle (works for four players on one screen), with gentle sway\n78\t  and a hold-breath button for long shots across a valley.\n79\t- **Hills, cover and light**: animals vanish behind ridges, trunks and fog banks; dawn mist, dusk backlight, snowfall.\n80\t\n81\t### 1.4 Do not copy\n82\tNo names, logos, characters, art, music, sounds, bonus-round concepts, levels or look from Big Buck Hunter. Our title,\n83\tcast, hunting grounds, bonus games, writing and art are our own. Reticle colors are the AI Hub seat colors (red, blue,\n84\tgreen, gold), not the cabinet's gun colors. No gore: hits throw puffs of fur and dust; animals fold up and lie down.\n85\t\n86\t---\n87\t\n88\t## 2. Look, sound and tone\n89\t\n90\t**Art direction: the engine's native retro-modern pixel style** (its Look target: PS1/N64-era 2D, shaded with\n91\t`E.tones`, outlined pixel art, the 5x7 font, `E.font.title` logos). Internal resolution `res: [480, 270]` with\n92\t`expand: true` (a 16:9 screen shows 480 x 270; wider screens show more landscape). The world is drawn by our own\n93\tpseudo-3D renderer (section 5.3) in the engine's pixels: a heightfield landscape a column at a time like the early-90s\n94\tvoxel terrain games, with billboards (trees, rocks, animals) scaled by distance.\n95\t\n96\tNew art made for this game: every animal and critter (procedural rigs), antlers, hunting props (tree stand, ground\n97\tblind frame, canoe bow, paper targets, trap house, scarecrow Hank, snowmen, farm dressing: barn, porch, corn rows),\n98\tthe Lodge, the logo, glyph icons, HUD pieces. Generic props come from the engine's library and asset store (pines, broadleaf\n99\ttrees, bushes, rocks, stumps, logs, fences, hay bales, tents, signposts, campfire rings: see 5.3.6).\n100\t\n101\tPer-hunt palettes:\n102\t- **Hollow Creek** (autumn morning): pale blue sky to peach horizon, low eastern sun, olive and ochre grass, russet\n103\t  leaf litter, orange oaks, red maples, white birches, dark teal creek with white riffles, valley mist.\n104\t- **Bugle Ridge** (alpine dusk): deep blue to orange-pink sky, sun setting behind the peaks (backlit animals with warm\n105\t  rim outlines), gold aspens, dark spruce, grey talus, gold-green meadow, a lake below catching the sunset.\n106\t- **Muskeg Lake** (first snow, overcast): grey-blue sky, falling snow, white and blue-grey ground, black spruce, orange\n107\t  tamaracks, brown reeds and cattails, dark water with ice at the edges, dense far fog.\n108\t\n109\t**Tone and writing**: like the source, short, loud and folksy. The emcee barks in all caps (\"BUCKS ONLY!\", \"HEART\n110\tSHOT!\", \"DOE! YOU'RE OUT!\"); the guide is dry and homespun; the rivals are small-town braggarts. Lines stay under\n111\tabout 60 characters so they read at 10 feet. Sample lines are in 6.8 and 6.9; write the rest in the same voice.\n112\t\n113\t**Audio**: everything synthesized in code (engine chip synth plus small Web Audio graphs). Bluegrass-flavored chiptune\n114\tmusic (banjo-roll pulse arpeggios, fiddle square leads, triangle bass, washboard noise drums), nature ambience, layered\n115\tgunshots with valley echoes, animal calls, Animalese/Simlish gibberish voices. Section 5.20.\n116\t\n117\t---\n118\t\n119\t## 3. Setup, folders and build\n120\t\n121\t### 3.1 Getting the engine and the hub client\n122\tThe plan was written against **engine handoff v0.85.2 (my-3d2dge commit 4390a98)** and **AI Hub lib/hub (ai-hub commit\n123\tf8835e4, session client 1.4.0)**. A copy fetched while planning is at\n124\t`/home/micha/.cache/x2e02c0-plan-research/{engine,hub}` (sparse clones: `engine/dist/handoff/`, `hub/lib/hub/`). Copy\n125\tfrom it, or clone again the same way into a new, empty folder inside the dev subfolder:\n126\t\n127\t```sh\n128\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git vendor/engine-src\n129\tgit -C vendor/engine-src sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\n130\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub.git vendor/hub-src\n131\tgit -C vendor/hub-src sparse-checkout set lib/hub\n132\t```\n133\t\n134\tRead in the handoff: `README.md`, `API.md` (whole), `AI_GUIDE.md` (as needed), `docs/ASSETS.md` (whole, before\n135\tdrawing props), `API-INDEX.md` (grep only), `modules/mode7.js` header (our renderer borrows its view cache and its\n136\tBackdrop-turning trick), `modules/arcade.js` header, `kits/kart.js` (Mode 7 usage), `game/` (template and its test).\n137\tIn the hub: `README.md` (whole), `examples/ghost.html` (the pattern we follow), `harness.html`.\n138\t\n139\t### 3.2 Environment notes (this machine)\n140\t- `/tmp` is a nearly full tmpfs (99-100% used by others). Point scratch space at the dev folder: `export"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67077d04-87f0-4158-a4f5-843d0a0e9941","uuid":"a02bf8b7-3bdd-4f4c-8209-9b0b5a3a60de","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:26:07.704Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174813__x2e02c0/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Big Buck Hunt-Off\n\nBuild plan for the developers who will make this game from `TASK.md` and this file alone. Read `TASK.md` first, then this\nplan top to bottom once; after that, use it as a reference. Everything here is a decision unless it says \"optional\".\nWhere a number is given, it is the starting default (a dev slider where marked), to be tuned in play.\n\n---\n\n## 0. At a glance\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| **Title** | **Big Buck Hunt-Off** (on screen: `BIG BUCK HUNT-OFF`). Picked as TASK.md asks: 50 riffs on \"Big Buck Hunter Pro\" brainstormed, one drawn with Node's `crypto.randomInt` (it drew #33 of 50). |\n| **Dev subfolder** | `BigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174813/` (GameTitle and SOURCE in PascalCase with spaces and punctuation dropped) |\n| **Deliverable** | `BigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174813.html` in this folder's root (beside `PLAN.md`), plus `exit-interview.json` beside it |\n| **Genre** | Arcade light-gun hunting shooter (a shooting gallery in a living landscape), with party versus and bonus mini-games |\n| **Perspective** | First person, on rails: a fixed vantage per site with scripted camera pans over a pseudo-3D heightfield landscape. Plus a slow-motion bullet cam on trophy kills, side-on x-ray Shot Chart cards in the tallies, and 2D screens for menus and the Lodge |\n| **Players** | 1 to 4 on one screen (simultaneous, competitive); 1 to 4 online in AI Hub sessions (ghost mode) |\n| **Engine** | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff package (full engine `my-3d2dge.js`), extended with new modules |\n\n**Pitch.** It's the 41st Annual Big Buck Hunt-Off at Whisker Lake Lodge. You sit in a tree stand, a blind or a canoe and\nlook out over a landscape that lives: deer, elk and moose wander, graze, stop and bolt across creek fords, cornfields,\nalpine meadows and frozen bogs. Pick out the bucks (only the bucks), pump your rifle between shots, and go for clean,\nlong, running shots for big points. Shoot a doe and you sit out the rest of the site. After each site an x-ray Shot\nChart shows exactly where every round went. Each hunt ends with a legendary trophy animal and a silly bonus round, and\nyour total climbs the Hunt-Off standings against the lodge regulars. Friends can play on the same couch with their own\nreticles, or online through AI Hub.\n\n**Core loop (one sentence, put it at the top of the game code):** spot the bucks, shoot clean, pump, never shoot a doe;\nbag them before they're gone, then read your shots in x-ray and climb the standings.\n\n**Session lengths:** Hunt-Off campaign about 17 minutes (3 hunts); a single hunt about 6 minutes; Party Quick Draw\nabout 5 minutes; an AI Hub round 3 minutes; Practice Range about 3 minutes.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The source: what to capture, what not to copy\n\n### 1.1 What research confirmed about Big Buck Hunter Pro\n\nResearch done while planning: Wikipedia's Big Buck Hunter article and web search results (Giant Bomb, MobyGames and\nretailer listings, quoted in search summaries; Giant Bomb and TV Tropes pages could not be fetched). Write \"unknown\"\nin the reports for anything below that is not listed as confirmed.\n\n- Arcade, released January 2006; designed by Play Mechanix, produced and distributed by Raw Thrills. Confirmed.\n- A two-gun cabinet with head-to-head play; the guns are pump-action (\"pump to reload\" is a speech call). Confirmed.\n- Shoot the males (bucks, bulls) as they run across the screen; a player who shoots a female is out until the next\n  site. Confirmed.\n- A hunt is split into five sites called treks, and a bonus round ends the session; a round ends when every male is\n  shot or some escape. Confirmed.\n- Points depend on distance, the animal's weight and accuracy. Bucks go down to a head shot, a heart shot, or by being\n  \"peppered\". Confirmed.\n- About 5 huntable animals, 15 bonus rounds and 12 critters that appear alongside the main animals for bonus points\n  (squirrels, rabbits, possums, skunks, raccoons, foxes, mountain lions, birds; sources disagree). Hunts are grouped by\n  animal (\"Whitetail Adventure\", \"Elk Adventure\"). Monthly national tournaments. Confirmed in part.\n- Tone: folksy, tongue-in-cheek rural humor with goofy bonus rounds and loud announcer calls. Confirmed in spirit\n  (bonus round names found); exact announcer lines are unknown.\n\n### 1.2 Distinctive elements, turned into our mechanics\n\n| Source element | Our version (original) |\n|---|---|\n| Two pump guns, head to head | Up to 4 simultaneous reticles, each with its own pump-action rifle; shared animals, first hit takes it |\n| Bucks only, a doe puts you out | Bucks/bulls are targets; does, fawns and calves are protected. Classic rule: shoot one and you're benched for the site (a Casual setting turns it into a points penalty) |\n| Sites (treks) and a bonus round | 3 hunts, each with 3 sites, a legendary **Trophy** encounter and a bonus game, all on one landscape per hunt with walk-in camera moves between vantages |\n| Distance, weight and accuracy scoring | Points = species base (from antler size) × distance × hit zone × running bonus, plus accuracy, clean sweep and critter bonuses |\n| Head and heart shots | Real 3D hit zones on every animal (heart, lungs, head, neck, shoulder, gut, ham, legs, antlers), shown afterwards on an **x-ray Shot Chart** |\n| Critters for bonus points | 12 critter designs across the hunts; some protected (skunk, Bessie the cow, eagle, loons) with comic penalties |\n| Goofy bonus rounds | Crow Patrol, Saucer Season, Snowball Standoff (ours) |\n| Tournaments | The Hunt-Off campaign against rival regulars; AI Hub rounds as an online Hunt-Off |\n| Pump to reload | Pump (rack) after every shot; when the tube is empty, the pump reloads it |\n| Loud announcer | An emcee with short all-caps barks, plus a folksy guide and rivals who talk in Animalese gibberish with captions |\n\n### 1.3 Additions that make it a better game\n- **The call** (grunt, bugle, moose grunt): a limited button that makes nearby animals stop and look for a moment: a\n  skill shot setup, and the key to two trophies.\n- **The scope**: a round magnifying lens around your reticle (works for four players on one screen), with gentle sway\n  and a hold-breath button for long shots across a valley.\n- **Hills, cover and light**: animals vanish behind ridges, trunks and fog banks; dawn mist, dusk backlight, snowfall.\n\n### 1.4 Do not copy\nNo names, logos, characters, art, music, sounds, bonus-round concepts, levels or look from Big Buck Hunter. Our title,\ncast, hunting grounds, bonus games, writing and art are our own. Reticle colors are the AI Hub seat colors (red, blue,\ngreen, gold), not the cabinet's gun colors. No gore: hits throw puffs of fur and dust; animals fold up and lie down.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Look, sound and tone\n\n**Art direction: the engine's native retro-modern pixel style** (its Look target: PS1/N64-era 2D, shaded with\n`E.tones`, outlined pixel art, the 5x7 font, `E.font.title` logos). Internal resolution `res: [480, 270]` with\n`expand: true` (a 16:9 screen shows 480 x 270; wider screens show more landscape). The world is drawn by our own\npseudo-3D renderer (section 5.3) in the engine's pixels: a heightfield landscape a column at a time like the early-90s\nvoxel terrain games, with billboards (trees, rocks, animals) scaled by distance.\n\nNew art made for this game: every animal and critter (procedural rigs), antlers, hunting props (tree stand, ground\nblind frame, canoe bow, paper targets, trap house, scarecrow Hank, snowmen, farm dressing: barn, porch, corn rows),\nthe Lodge, the logo, glyph icons, HUD pieces. Generic props come from the engine's library and asset store (pines, broadleaf\ntrees, bushes, rocks, stumps, logs, fences, hay bales, tents, signposts, campfire rings: see 5.3.6).\n\nPer-hunt palettes:\n- **Hollow Creek** (autumn morning): pale blue sky to peach horizon, low eastern sun, olive and ochre grass, russet\n  leaf litter, orange oaks, red maples, white birches, dark teal creek with white riffles, valley mist.\n- **Bugle Ridge** (alpine dusk): deep blue to orange-pink sky, sun setting behind the peaks (backlit animals with warm\n  rim outlines), gold aspens, dark spruce, grey talus, gold-green meadow, a lake below catching the sunset.\n- **Muskeg Lake** (first snow, overcast): grey-blue sky, falling snow, white and blue-grey ground, black spruce, orange\n  tamaracks, brown reeds and cattails, dark water with ice at the edges, dense far fog.\n\n**Tone and writing**: like the source, short, loud and folksy. The emcee barks in all caps (\"BUCKS ONLY!\", \"HEART\nSHOT!\", \"DOE! YOU'RE OUT!\"); the guide is dry and homespun; the rivals are small-town braggarts. Lines stay under\nabout 60 characters so they read at 10 feet. Sample lines are in 6.8 and 6.9; write the rest in the same voice.\n\n**Audio**: everything synthesized in code (engine chip synth plus small Web Audio graphs). Bluegrass-flavored chiptune\nmusic (banjo-roll pulse arpeggios, fiddle square leads, triangle bass, washboard noise drums), nature ambience, layered\ngunshots with valley echoes, animal calls, Animalese/Simlish gibberish voices. Section 5.20.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Setup, folders and build\n\n### 3.1 Getting the engine and the hub client\nThe plan was written against **engine handoff v0.85.2 (my-3d2dge commit 4390a98)** and **AI Hub lib/hub (ai-hub commit\nf8835e4, session client 1.4.0)**. A copy fetched while planning is at\n`/home/micha/.cache/x2e02c0-plan-research/{engine,hub}` (sparse clones: `engine/dist/handoff/`, `hub/lib/hub/`). Copy\nfrom it, or clone again the same way into a new, empty folder inside the dev subfolder:\n\n```sh\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git vendor/engine-src\ngit -C vendor/engine-src sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub.git vendor/hub-src\ngit -C vendor/hub-src sparse-checkout set lib/hub\n```\n\nRead in the handoff: `README.md`, `API.md` (whole), `AI_GUIDE.md` (as needed), `docs/ASSETS.md` (whole, before\ndrawing props), `API-INDEX.md` (grep only), `modules/mode7.js` header (our renderer borrows its view cache and its\nBackdrop-turning trick), `modules/arcade.js` header, `kits/kart.js` (Mode 7 usage), `game/` (template and its test).\nIn the hub: `README.md` (whole), `examples/ghost.html` (the pattern we follow), `harness.html`.\n\n### 3.2 Environment notes (this machine)\n- `/tmp` is a nearly full tmpfs (99-100% used by others). Point scratch space at the dev folder: `export","numLines":140,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1416}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VXd85haawHhzcnyVAU94Lv","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
